```python
# --- Session-level corroboration: one claim, one window (Receipt #912) ---
#
# `classify_productivity` above asks a DAY-level question and keeps it: on any
# day where one session landed a task commit the day is in
# `days_with_task_commits`, so a SIBLING session on that same day that claimed
# success and produced nothing is invisible to it. With a 3-sessions/day
# cadence that is the common case, and it is the exact case the Day-196 design
# comment was written about. So this reader joins on the SESSION instead: each
# claim is checked against the session's OWN window, derived from the audit-log
# directory stamps, and the two readers are deliberately kept side by side --
# neither is a replacement for the other and the day-level check is untouched.

CLAIM_CORROBORATED = "corroborated"
CLAIM_NO_COMMITS = "claim-no-commits"
CLAIM_NO_CLAIM = "no-claim"
CLAIM_COULD_NOT_CHECK = "claim-could-not-check"
# THE FIFTH VALUE, FOLDED INTO NONE OF THE FOUR. An OPEN window is a different
# fact from both CORROBORATED (no commits yet, and that is not a miss) and
# COULD_NOT_CHECK (the window IS resolvable -- it simply has no closing bound
# yet). Folding it into either would make the flag fire on a session that has
# not finished committing, which is the confident-wrong-diagnosis defect.
#
# REACHABILITY, STATED RATHER THAN IMPLIED: this is the RENDERER'S FLOOR, in
# the same sense `observed_label_clause`'s empty branch is -- `SESSION_DIR` is
# created at session END (`scripts/evolve.sh` Step 7c2), so the newest audit
# directory read here is always a session that has already finished
# committing, and a live run reaches CORROBORATED instead. Kept because a
# session directory can exist before its commits do (a pushed-but-not-finished
# session, a hand-built fixture), and flagging it would be a false alarm on the
# one signal whose value is that it fires rarely.
CLAIM_OPEN_WINDOW = "claim-open-window"
```

